<ResourceDictionary xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
  <!--
    THE PRODUCT'S TOKEN LAYER. One file, and before it existed there were none: App.axaml was
    eight lines of bare FluentTheme, and across every .axaml under client/src there were zero
    ResourceDictionary, zero StaticResource and zero DynamicResource. Colour was pasted as hex at
    342 sites, 102 of them the same ink.

    THE SHAPE IS WPF'S OWN, deliberately and for one reason: the shipping product re-themes at
    runtime. `RefreshThemeAwareElements` rewrites `Application.Current.Resources` by KEY - the
    Colour entry and its Brush sibling, both - every time the active mod changes
    (WPF MainWindow/MainWindow.xaml.cs:1611-1682), and everything downstream repaints because it
    referenced the key rather than a value. So this file is Colours first, Brushes derived from
    them, and every consumer binds the BRUSH dynamically. Mod-driven re-theming is a separate
    capability and is NOT built here; this is the structure that makes it a small feature instead
    of a second sweep.

    ============================ THE VALUES, AND WHAT IS NOT DECIDED HERE ============================
    Every colour below carries the port's CURRENT measured value, and beside it the shipping
    product's value for the same role. They are not the same palette: the port is violet-tinted,
    upstream is navy-and-pink. This file does not change that, because the conversion that
    populated it was mechanical and value-preserving - nine files read these exact bytes back off
    real pixels or real markup (client/tools/verify/checks.json, RackPresentationTests,
    TrainerCardTests, TrainerCardRecordPresentationTests, CompanionPrivacyPresentationTests,
    NavigationShellHeadlessTests and their siblings), and a sweep that recoloured while it
    tokenised would have been two changes wearing one commit.

    WHAT IT DOES BUY: the palette is now ONE LINE PER ROLE. Flipping the shell onto upstream's
    palette is editing the values in this block and re-deriving the tolerances in checks.json -
    a bounded, reviewable change instead of 342 scattered edits.

    THE ONE EXCEPTION, and it is the reason this file exists at all: FLUENT'S ACCENT FAMILY at
    the foot of this file had NO port value to preserve. It resolved from the user's Windows
    personalisation setting, so the product's checkboxes and sliders were Windows blue in a
    magenta app AND A DIFFERENT COLOUR ON EVERY MACHINE. There is nothing to conserve in that,
    so it takes the shipping product's accent verbatim.
  -->

  <!-- ================================= GROUNDS AND SURFACES ================================= -->

  <!-- The application ground: the shell window's own Background, and the page ground behind
       every card. WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:9 DarkerBg #FF121220. -->
  <Color x:Key="DarkerBg">#FF141018</Color>

  <!-- Structural chrome that frames content rather than carrying it: the nav rail, the Studio
       rack, the diagnostic footer, the session action bar.
       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:10 SurfaceBg #FF181830. -->
  <Color x:Key="SurfaceBg">#FF19141F</Color>

  <!-- A card that carries content: the module panel, the Play hero.
       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:7 PanelBg #FF1C1C35. -->
  <Color x:Key="PanelBg">#FF1B1622</Color>

  <!-- A seat a control sits in: a rail door at rest, a companion dial seat. Upstream has no
       distinct key for this tier - it seats controls on SurfaceBg - so the port keeps its own. -->
  <Color x:Key="SeatBg">#FF1E1822</Color>

  <!-- One step above a panel: a notice, a badge, a hovered rack row.
       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:71 ElevatedSurface #FF222240. -->
  <Color x:Key="ElevatedSurface">#FF241E2A</Color>

  <!-- The interactive tier: a selected row's fill, a secondary button's face, a progress track.
       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:19 PanelAccent #FF2E2E4A. -->
  <Color x:Key="PanelAccent">#FF2A2130</Color>

  <!-- A border at rest, and the hairline between chrome regions.
       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:20 PanelAccentHover #FF3A3A5C. -->
  <Color x:Key="PanelAccentHover">#FF3A2F3E</Color>

  <!-- ======================================== INK ======================================== -->

  <!-- Primary ink. The single most-pasted literal in the product before this file existed: 102
       sites, 48 of them in StudioPage.axaml alone.
       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:11 TextLight #FFF0F0F5. -->
  <Color x:Key="TextLight">#FFE8E0EE</Color>

  <!-- Secondary ink: description lines, units, hints.
       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:15 TextMuted #FFA0A0BC. -->
  <Color x:Key="TextMuted">#FF9A8FA3</Color>

  <!-- Decorative-only, and it stays that way: never carry information in TextDim. Also the
       pointer-over border and the rack's off/armed state dot.
       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:74 TextDim #FF7A7A94. -->
  <Color x:Key="TextDim">#FF6B5B73</Color>

  <!-- NO TOKEN FOR WHITE, and that is a decision rather than an omission. The product paints
       #FFFFFFFF at five sites and they do not share a role: three are ink on an accent fill, one
       is the Play hero's title on a dark card, one is a picker handle. A "TextOnAccent" key would
       have to be pasted at the two that are not on an accent to keep the sweep mechanical, and a
       token whose name is wrong at 40% of its uses is worse than the literal it replaced. White
       is not a palette decision; it is white. -->

  <!-- ====================================== ACCENTS ======================================

       THREE accent colours, because the product really paints three, and naming what is there
       beats inventing a scale nothing uses.

       For the later mod-re-theme capability: upstream's rewrite targets the key `PinkColor`
       (WPF MainWindow/MainWindow.xaml.cs:1655), so a mod reaching into this dictionary by that
       name repaints the Fluent accent and the info toast. The shell's own two accents are
       separate keys and would need naming in that feature's own mapping - recorded here so that
       work discovers it from the file rather than from a capture.
  -->

  <!-- Upstream's accent, verbatim, and already the port's own value at the info toast
       (ToastHost.axaml) and the Studio picker. This is also what Fluent's accent family below
       resolves to. WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:5 PinkColor #FFFF69B4 - EXACT MATCH. -->
  <Color x:Key="PinkColor">#FFFF69B4</Color>

  <!-- The shell's accent: the session START button's idle livery, the module action rim, the
       intake gate. WPF's own START is PinkBrush (WPF MainWindow/MainWindow.StartStop.cs:779),
       so the ROLE matches PinkColor above and only the value diverges.
       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:5 PinkColor #FFFF69B4. -->
  <Color x:Key="ShellAccent">#FFD05CE8</Color>

  <!-- The bright accent: SELECTION and LIVE. Rail door checked border, rack row selection
       marker, the rack's live state dot.

       THIS COLOUR IS THE HEADED HARNESS'S SEEDED-REGRESSION ANCHOR and this file is now where
       it lives: client/tools/verify/self-test.ps1 replaces the value on the line below with a
       wrong one, rebuilds, and requires BOTH `rail-door-selected-border` and
       `rack-row-selected-marker` to trip on real pixels. It used to seed MainWindow.axaml, where
       the same literal appeared at three sites; seeding the token is strictly stronger, because
       every present and future surface that selects reads this one key.
       client/tools/verify/checks.json reads it back as #E066FF. Do not inline it back into a
       surface, and do not write the seed value anywhere in this file - the self-test proves its
       own restore by requiring the seed to be absent afterwards.
       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:66 PinkVibrant #FFFF5CA8. -->
  <Color x:Key="ShellAccentBright">#FFE066FF</Color>

  <!-- The tier-2 / tier-3 entitlement rim and its refusal plate.
       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:129 NeonPurple #FFB47BFF - EXACT MATCH. -->
  <Color x:Key="NeonPurple">#FFB47BFF</Color>

  <!-- ===================================== SEMANTICS =====================================
       Severity, not brand. These stay put when the accent moves, exactly as upstream's Danger
       and SuccessGreen do (WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:23-28 say so in their own words).
  -->

  <!-- A session that is RUNNING repaints the one START button red. Upstream builds this in code
       as Color.FromRgb(255,107,107) (WPF MainWindow/MainWindow.StartStop.cs:756) - so the value
       is upstream's exactly, and only the key is the port's.
       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:23 Danger #FFE53935 is upstream's other red, for dialogs. -->
  <Color x:Key="Danger">#FFFF6B6B</Color>

  <!-- The FAILURE livery, which exists to be told apart at a glance from the two REFUSAL
       liveries above. Upstream never needs it: its failure surface is a modal MessageBox with
       MessageBoxImage.Warning (WPF MainWindow/MainWindow.Lab.cs:164-165), a different WINDOW.
       The port reuses the plate idiom on the same page, so the severity signal has to be
       carried by the colour instead of by the window. -->
  <Color x:Key="Warning">#FFF0A02E</Color>

  <!-- ====================================== BRUSHES ======================================
       Derived from the colours above through DynamicResource, which is upstream's own structure
       and its own stated reason: "use DynamicResource so runtime color changes propagate"
       (WPF Resources/Theme/Brushes.xaml:4). Consumers bind the BRUSH; nothing in the product
       binds a Color except a gradient stop.
  -->

  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DarkerBgBrush" Color="{DynamicResource DarkerBg}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceBgBrush" Color="{DynamicResource SurfaceBg}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PanelBgBrush" Color="{DynamicResource PanelBg}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SeatBgBrush" Color="{DynamicResource SeatBg}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ElevatedSurfaceBrush" Color="{DynamicResource ElevatedSurface}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PanelAccentBrush" Color="{DynamicResource PanelAccent}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PanelAccentHoverBrush" Color="{DynamicResource PanelAccentHover}" />

  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextLightBrush" Color="{DynamicResource TextLight}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextMutedBrush" Color="{DynamicResource TextMuted}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextDimBrush" Color="{DynamicResource TextDim}" />

  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PinkBrush" Color="{DynamicResource PinkColor}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ShellAccentBrush" Color="{DynamicResource ShellAccent}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ShellAccentBrightBrush" Color="{DynamicResource ShellAccentBright}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NeonPurpleBrush" Color="{DynamicResource NeonPurple}" />

  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DangerBrush" Color="{DynamicResource Danger}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WarningBrush" Color="{DynamicResource Warning}" />

  <!-- ==================================== TYPOGRAPHY ====================================
       The product had NO FontFamily anywhere before this line: six occurrences across the whole
       tree, every one a Consolas probe inside a web-host window. Upstream sets Segoe UI on the
       Window and inherits it everywhere (WPF MainWindow/MainWindow.xaml:24).

       THE FALLBACK CHAIN IS NOT DECORATION. Segoe UI does not exist on Linux, and this client
       ships there. Noto Sans and DejaVu Sans are the two faces a desktop Linux install can be
       relied on to carry; the trailing generic hands the last word to the platform's own font
       manager rather than to whatever Avalonia happens to pick first.
  -->
  <FontFamily x:Key="CcpFontFamily">Segoe UI, Noto Sans, DejaVu Sans, sans-serif</FontFamily>

  <!-- ================================== GEOMETRY SCALE ==================================
       The radii the shell really uses, stated once. Adopted by the shell's own style block
       (Views/MainWindow.axaml); per-page one-off geometry is still written inline.
  -->
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusChip">4</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusControl">6</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusPanel">8</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusPlate">10</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusGate">12</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusPill">14</CornerRadius>
  <!-- 15 is 16 minus the card's 1px border, which is where upstream derives it
       (WPF Views/Tabs/PlayTabView.xaml:252). A band inside RadiusCard must use this. -->
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusBand">15</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusCard">16</CornerRadius>

  <!-- The uniform paddings. Non-uniform ones stay inline: a "12,6" is a layout decision at one
       site, not a token, and hiding it behind a name would cost a lookup and buy nothing. -->
  <Thickness x:Key="PadRack">8</Thickness>
  <Thickness x:Key="PadNotice">12</Thickness>
  <Thickness x:Key="PadPanel">16</Thickness>
  <Thickness x:Key="PadCard">24</Thickness>

  <!-- ============================== FLUENT'S ACCENT FAMILY ==============================

       THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES, measured on captured pixels before it existed: every unstyled
       Fluent control resolved its accent from the user's Windows personalisation setting
       (HKCU\...\Explorer\Accent\AccentColorMenu). The port's checkboxes and sliders measured
       #0078D4 - 3,500 exact-hex pixels in a port capture and ZERO in the same capture of the
       shipping product - so the most-touched controls in a magenta application were Windows
       blue, and a different blue on every machine.

       Application.Resources is consulted BEFORE the theme's own dictionaries, so declaring the
       seven keys here shadows Fluent's without forking a control theme or restating a template.
       All seven are written out rather than left to Fluent's shade calculator: the calculator
       runs off the platform accent, which is the thing being removed.

       The shades are the standard Fluent ladder around the base - Light1/2/3 lift toward white,
       Dark1/2/3 toward black - so hover, pressed and disabled states stay distinguishable
       instead of collapsing onto one flat pink.

       WPF Resources/Theme/Colors.xaml:5 PinkColor #FFFF69B4 is the base, and upstream paints the
       same accent on the same controls: its 18-unit circle checkbox takes PinkBrush on hover and
       on check (WPF Resources/Theme/Inputs.xaml:218-262).
  -->
  <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColor">#FFFF69B4</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColorLight1">#FFFF85C2</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColorLight2">#FFFFA1D0</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColorLight3">#FFFFBDDE</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColorDark1">#FFD9599A</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColorDark2">#FFB34A80</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColorDark3">#FF8C3A66</Color>

</ResourceDictionary>
